        definition: "The theological concept of continuous creation: not a single originating act at the beginning of time but an ongoing creative sustaining of existence at every moment. God, on this account, is not primarily first cause but the continuous ground of being — the creative source from which existence is perpetually renewed. Without this continuous creative act, things would not merely stop changing; they would cease to be. Creatio continua shifts the emphasis from creation as a past event to creation as the present condition of all existence: the world is always being made, never simply given. This connects directly to Whitehead's cosmology, to autopoiesis, and to the contemplative sense that reality is a continuous creative event rather than a settled background.",
